    "body": "Great on any Friday: Apple USB-C Woven Cable - 3ft, 60W, White\nPorsche Unveils New and Discreet Dashcam Packing High-Res Recording With HDR\n9 photos\nPhoto: Porsche\nIn case you didn't know, among the endless accessories you can Purchase as a Porsche owner (or even just a fan), there's also a dedicated dashcam, which has now crossed over into a new generation. It's a multi-camera system that automatically records relevant driving and parking events, and it does all that in HDR, so that you can see exactly what just occurred.\nThere are plenty of reasons why you might want to install a dashcam in your car, and you don't have to own a Porsche in order to want to do so. You could be the best driver in the world and still find yourself in a situation where a dashcam would have come in handy. Besides, it's even more frustrating when something happens to your car when you're not around.\nThen there's the matter of aesthetics. I don't think you want to spend six figures on a sports car only to stick a generic piece of plastic to your windshield. A lot of dashcams are extremely unaesthetic. You also don't want it obstructing your field of view in any way, but maybe that's just a pet peeve of mine. I don't even want any scented ornaments hanging from the rearview mirror.\nNow, Porsche's new dashcam design is exactly what the doctor ordered, if what you're looking for is a discreet piece of tech. It also records what's happening inside the passenger compartment, which the previous model wasn't able to do.\nThe new device is positioned directly bellow/behind the interior rearview mirror, so it shouldn't interfere with your visibility . It even features an illuminated Porsche logo so that nobody can \"accuse\" you of having bought a generic aftermarket camera.\nAs far as image quality is concerned, we're dealing with QHD recordings with HDR tech, and there's also a polarizing filter that deals with reflections, which by the way, can be a problem on sunny days. Depending on the angle of the sun and the materials inside the vehicle, reflections on the windshield can render certain details in a recording more or less illegible - and being able to make out a license plate is one of the main selling points of any dashcam.\nPhoto: Porsche According to Porsche , the camera will produce clear, detailed footage even at higher speeds, which is important given the horsepower figures usually available to Porsche drivers. Then there's the G-sensor and radar, which detect events automatically and triggers a recording. Additional GPS information documents the exact location when the recording begins.\nThere are, of course, limitations regarding which cameras you can install on your Porsche. Owners of Cabriolet and Targa models will have to settle for just the front-facing unit, for obvious reasons.\nAs for connectivity, users can access all recordings, review events and adjust settings all from their My Porsche app.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche tech dashcam porsche accessories technology gadgets\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Sergiu Tudose\nSergiu got to experience both American and European car \"scenes\" at an early age (his father drove a Ford Fiesta XR2 supermini in the 80s). After spending over 15 years at local and international auto publications, he's starting to appreciate comfort behind the wheel more than raw power and acceleration. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tucker, Built After Factory Shutdown, Sat Hidden for 13 Years. Great-Grandsons Finally Bring It Home",
